WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
Neurons, layers, and parameters.

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:10.200
A neuron with inputs x1 through xn computes tanh(w1 x1 + … + wn xn + b).

3
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.299
Its weights and bias are its parameters, the numbers training adjusts.

4
00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:19.656
A layer of m such neurons maps a vector in R^n to a vector in R^m.

5
00:00:19.936 --> 00:00:29.451
Stack layers and you have an MLP: MLP(3, [4, 4, 1]) maps 3 inputs through two hidden layers of 4 neurons to a single output.

6
00:00:29.731 --> 00:00:38.435
Mathematically, a layer is x maps to tanh(W x + b), with W an m-by-n matrix applied elementwise through tanh.

7
00:00:38.715 --> 00:00:51.024
The scalar version in micrograd builds that matrix product out of individual multiply and add nodes, which is why it is slow and why it is so good for learning: you can inspect every single number.

8
00:00:51.304 --> 00:00:52.605
Why the nonlinearity?

9
00:00:52.885 --> 00:01:00.864
Without it, a stack of layers collapses to a single affine map (a product of matrices is a matrix), so depth would add nothing.

10
00:01:01.144 --> 00:01:05.219
With it, the MLP can approximate a much richer family of functions.

11
00:01:05.499 --> 00:01:15.376
And tanh in particular squashes outputs into the interval from minus 1 to 1, which matches the targets of plus or minus 1 in the video’s tiny dataset.
